you haven't experienced before, then

00:19:33.760 --> 00:19:38.080
you're just a fortune teller. Mind trap

00:19:35.840 --> 00:19:39.600
number 13. Should statements. I should

00:19:38.080 --> 00:19:40.640
have done this. I should have done that.

00:19:39.600 --> 00:19:42.880
Should have, could have, would have

00:19:40.640 --> 00:19:44.720
statements are the rules that you create

00:19:42.880 --> 00:19:46.880
for yourself that nobody can actually

00:19:44.720 --> 00:19:48.880
follow because you didn't do it. It just

00:19:46.880 --> 00:19:50.799
didn't happen. Sometimes I'm guilty of

00:19:48.880 --> 00:19:52.880
this and it is so annoying when I say

00:19:50.799 --> 00:19:53.919
out loud like I annoy myself when I say

00:19:52.880 --> 00:19:56.400
things like, "Oh, I should have done

00:19:53.919 --> 00:19:58.160
this sooner." It is a form of judging

00:19:56.400 --> 00:20:00.480
ourselves where we walk around with this

00:19:58.160 --> 00:20:02.400
invisible framework that says, "I should

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:03.520
always do the right thing and say the

00:20:02.400 --> 00:20:05.360
right things and I shouldn't need

00:20:03.520 --> 00:20:07.440
anyone's help or I should just be able

00:20:05.360 --> 00:20:09.360
to do it by myself." It feels like it's

00:20:07.440 --> 00:20:11.280
reasonable, but if you say it to someone

00:20:09.360 --> 00:20:13.520
or write it down somewhere, you realize

00:20:11.280 --> 00:20:15.120
that these are all impossible standards.

00:20:13.520 --> 00:20:17.039
A lot of people who have been held to

00:20:15.120 --> 00:20:19.200
very high standards and haven't been

00:20:17.039 --> 00:20:21.039
able to meet them do suffer from this

00:20:19.200 --> 00:20:22.960
mind trap. This is a form of

00:20:21.039 --> 00:20:24.799
self-punishment that's disguised as

00:20:22.960 --> 00:20:26.240
having high standards. And we should all

00:20:24.799 --> 00:20:27.360
know the difference. When you catch

00:20:26.240 --> 00:20:29.760
yourself in the should, could have,

00:20:27.360 --> 00:20:32.159
would statement, try and reframe it and

00:20:29.760 --> 00:20:33.679
replace it with what's actually true. I

00:20:32.159 --> 00:20:36.080
wish I were further along in this

00:20:33.679 --> 00:20:37.760
project is more honest than saying, I

00:20:36.080 --> 00:20:40.080
should be further along. You can also

00:20:37.760 --> 00:20:42.159
reframe your thoughts to what can I do

00:20:40.080 --> 00:20:45.039
about this situation from here on out?

00:20:42.159 --> 00:20:47.200
or I'm glad I started today. Mind trap

00:20:45.039 --> 00:20:49.679
number 14, labeling. And this is the

00:20:47.200 --> 00:20:51.760
final cognitive distortion. When you're

00:20:49.679 --> 00:20:53.360
taking a behavior and you turn it into a

00:20:51.760 --> 00:20:55.120
whole identity or character profile,

00:20:53.360 --> 00:20:57.120
instead of saying, "Oops, I made a

00:20:55.120 --> 00:20:58.880
mistake. My bad." You say, "I am such an

00:20:57.120 --> 00:21:00.159
insufferable loser. I can't do anything

00:20:58.880 --> 00:21:02.400
right." Instead of saying, "Wow, that

00:21:00.159 --> 00:21:04.720
was a challenging day." You say, "I am a

00:21:02.400 --> 00:21:06.559
bad manager." Not only do we do it to

00:21:04.720 --> 00:21:08.720
ourselves, but to other people, too.

00:21:06.559 --> 00:21:10.720
When someone does something to you that

00:21:08.720 --> 00:21:12.240
you don't like, you may put a label on

00:21:10.720 --> 00:21:13.679
it. For example, if someone in your

00:21:12.240 --> 00:21:14.960
household forgets to do the dishes

00:21:13.679 --> 00:21:17.120
instead of thinking, "Oh, they will get

00:21:14.960 --> 00:21:18.799
to it later." It becomes they are lazy,

00:21:17.120 --> 00:21:20.159
they are dirty. If someone cuts you off

00:21:18.799 --> 00:21:21.440
in traffic, maybe it's not because

00:21:20.159 --> 00:21:23.440
they're in the middle of a life or death

00:21:21.440 --> 00:21:25.840
situation, but you're thinking, "Wow,

00:21:23.440 --> 00:21:27.760
they're the world's biggest a-ho right

00:21:25.840 --> 00:21:29.520
now." This labeling collapses the

00:21:27.760 --> 00:21:31.360
distance between what you do and who you

00:21:29.520 --> 00:21:34.240
are. It also removes any possibilities

00:21:31.360 --> 00:21:36.320
of reform, growth, and change. It does

00:21:34.240 --> 00:21:38.320
not allow for any kind of context.

00:21:36.320 --> 00:21:40.159
Immediately you are jumping into

00:21:38.320 --> 00:21:42.480
judgment and the judgment becomes

00:21:40.159 --> 00:21:45.360
someone's identity. She is a b word.

00:21:42.480 --> 00:21:47.760
He's a dword. It's just who they are.

00:21:45.360 --> 00:21:49.520
But is it really? You are not your worst

00:21:47.760 --> 00:21:52.000
moment or your worst thought and you're

00:21:49.520 --> 00:21:54.000
not a label. And nobody is either. We're

00:21:52.000 --> 00:21:56.240
all just people who have done things.

00:21:54.000 --> 00:21:58.480
And sometimes even those things don't

00:21:56.240 --> 00:22:00.240
define someone for life. Especially if

00:21:58.480 --> 00:22:02.400
there is room for acknowledgement and

00:22:00.240 --> 00:22:05.039
growth. If you want to label something,

00:22:02.400 --> 00:22:07.120
at least make it kinder and good. I can

00:22:05.039 --> 00:22:09.039
always find ways to do things. I am an

00:22:07.120 --> 00:22:10.720
explorer, a seeker of growth. And these

00:22:09.039 --> 00:22:12.080
are all great affirmations to have. I'm

00:22:10.720 --> 00:22:13.679
actually building a library, a whole

00:22:12.080 --> 00:22:15.039
collection of affirmations, meditation,

00:22:13.679 --> 00:22:16.480
subliminals, and other somatic

00:22:15.039 --> 00:22:17.840
techniques that I have used to heal my

